{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Eviction for criminal convictions or offenses (N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.1(o)-(r))","requirement":"A landlord may file for eviction when a tenant is convicted of, pleads guilty to, or is found civilly liable for assault, terrorist threats, theft, illegal drug activity or human trafficking on the premises, or knowingly houses such a person. A three-day Notice to Quit is required and suit must be filed within two years.","key_value":"3-day notice to quit; 2-year filing limit","coverage_conditions":"Tenants covered by the Eviction Law; the human trafficking ground covers leased premises, buildings, mobile home parks and land appurtenant.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":null},"exemptions":"The drug-activity ground does not apply to a tenant who allows a juvenile found delinquent for drug use or possession to reside at the property.","penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.1","quoted_span":"No eviction suit may be brought more than two years after: the juvenile was found to be delinquent; conviction of the person; or after the person’s release from incarceration, whichever is later. A Notice to Quit must be served on the tenant at least three day s prior to filing a suit for (N.J.S.A. 2A-18-61.1(o)).","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Eviction causes for landlord-occupied two- and three-family dwellings","requirement":"A tenant of a landlord-occupied two- or three-family dwelling may be removed only by court order, and only for holding over, nonpayment of rent, disorderly conduct, willful damage, constant violation of written rules, or breach of a lease re-entry clause.","key_value":"3-month, 1-month or 3-day notice to quit","coverage_conditions":"Tenants of landlord-occupied two- and three-family dwellings, including a cause for not renewing the lease with written notice for delivery of possession.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":2,"max_units":3,"owner_dependent":true,"other":null},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.2","quoted_span":"However, in these cases, the landlord must prove that the tenant (a) is staying after the expiration of the term of the lease, (b) is staying after a failure to pay rent, (c) is disorderly so as to destroy the peace and quiet of other tena nts, (d) willfully destroys or damages the premises, (e) constantly violates the written rules and regulations or (f) violates any lease provision where the lease reserves a right of re -entry for such violations.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Good-cause and notice rules for rooming and boarding house evictions","requirement":"Owners of rooming and boarding houses must follow the good causes and notice requirements of the Eviction Law when evicting residents, unless the Bureau orders otherwise, and must give the County Welfare Board at least three working days' notice before starting an eviction action.","key_value":"3 working days' notice to County Welfare Board","coverage_conditions":"Rooming and boarding houses: buildings with at least two living units occupied by unrelated persons without private kitchens and bathrooms, subject to the Act's exceptions.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":2,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"Rooming and boarding houses"},"exemptions":"Hotels with more than 85 percent temporary occupancy by people with homes elsewhere, school and college dormitories, buildings housing only college students, and certain residences for the disabled.","penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.A.C. 5:27-3.3","quoted_span":"The Regulations Governing Rooming and Boarding Houses, which are enforced by the Bureau of Rooming and Boarding House Standards of the Department of Community Affairs, require owners of rooming and boarding houses to follow the good causes and notice requirements of the Eviction Law when evicting residents, except if otherwise ordered by the Bureau (N.J.A.C. 5:27-3.3(c)).","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Penalties and occupancy duties after owner-occupancy or demolition evictions","requirement":"A landlord who evicted a tenant to personally occupy the unit must occupy it at least six months; a landlord who evicted to board up, demolish or retire the building must not use it residentially for five years. Violations bring treble damages plus fees and up to a $10,000 civil penalty.","key_value":"6 months' owner occupancy; 5-year bar on residential use; up to $10,000 civil penalty","coverage_conditions":"Landlords who gained possession after giving notice of personal occupancy or of permanent removal of the property from residential use.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":true,"other":null},"exemptions":null,"penalty":"Three times the damages suffered plus attorney fees and costs; a civil penalty up to $10,000 for each violation of the five-year residential-use bar.","effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.6","quoted_span":"When a tenant vacates a dwelling unit after having been given notice that the landlord wishes to personally occupy the unit the landlord must occupy the unit for at least six (6) months. If instead the landlord permits personal occupancy of the unit by another tenant or registration of conversion of the property to a condominium or cooperative, the landlord is liable to the former tenant for three (3) times the damages suffered plus attorney fees and costs (N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.6(a)).","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Reprisal protections for tenants exercising civil rights","requirement":"A landlord may not evict, alter a lease, or refuse to renew in reprisal for a tenant enforcing lease or law rights, making a good-faith health or safety complaint to a government authority, joining a tenant organization, or refusing a retaliatory lease change.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"All rental properties used for dwelling purposes, including mobile homes, except owner-occupied two- or three-family dwellings.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":true,"other":"All dwellings except owner-occupied two- and three-family dwellings"},"exemptions":"Owner-occupied two- or three-family dwellings.","penalty":"The tenant may sue the landlord for damages in a civil action.","effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:42-10.10","quoted_span":"A landlord cannot take reprisal action against a tenant by eviction, substantial alteration of a lease or its terms, or refusal to renew a lease when a tenant exercises certain civil rights (N.J.S.A. 2A:42-10.10). The law against reprisal applies to all rental properties used for dwelling purposes, including mobile homes, except owner-occupied two- or three-family dwellings.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Senior Citizens and Disabled Protected Tenancy Act","requirement":"Qualified senior citizens, permanently disabled tenants, and certain disabled veterans who lived in a building being converted to condominium, cooperative or fee simple ownership at least one year before the conversion recording may be protected from eviction for 40 years.","key_value":"40 years' protection; income limit 3 times county average per-person income or $50,000, whichever is greater","coverage_conditions":"Tenants at least 62 before the conversion recording, permanently disabled tenants, or honorably discharged service-disabled veterans (60% or higher) living in the building at least one year before the conversion recording date.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"Buildings converted to condominium, cooperative or fee simple ownership"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.22","quoted_span":"Tenants may be protected from eviction for 40 years if their family income is not more than three (3) times the average per person income in their county or $50,000.00, whichever is greater (N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.28).","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Tenant Protection Act of 1992 protected tenancy (Hudson County)","requirement":"The Tenant Protection Law of 1992 extends protected tenancy to qualified tenants in qualified counties — at present only Hudson County — in buildings converted or being converted who were not eligible as senior citizens or disabled persons under the 1981 Act.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Tenants in buildings converted or being converted in qualified counties; the only qualified county at present is Hudson County.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"Qualified counties, currently only Hudson County"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.40","quoted_span":"The Tenant Protection Law of 1992 amendment extends protections to qualified tenants in qualified counties in buildings converted or being converted who were not eligible for Protected Tenancy as either Senior Citizens or Disabled Persons under the “Senior Citizens and Disabled Protected Tenancy Act of 1981” (N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.40 to -61.59; N.J.A.C. 5:24-3.1 to -3.4). At the present time, the only qualified county is Hudson County (N.J.A.C. 5:24-3.2(b)).","interaction":null,"confidence":0.75,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Tenant protections and notice in foreclosure","requirement":"Foreclosure alone is not grounds for eviction; a buyer who purchases a foreclosed property to personally occupy it must give the tenant a 90-day notice to quit, and a new owner must notify tenants in English and Spanish within 10 business days after the sale.","key_value":"90-day notice to quit; 10 business days' notice after sale","coverage_conditions":"Residential tenants in foreclosed properties; month-to-month tenants and owner-occupied two- and three-family units are not exempt from the 90-day notice requirement.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"Residential rental units in foreclosed properties"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:50-69","quoted_span":"Before a tenant can be evicted due to foreclosure, the landlord must provide the tenant with a 90 -day notice to qui t when the foreclosed property has been purchased by a buyer who wants to personally occupy it as his or her primary residence.","interaction":"Federal law does not preempt State or local law that provides longer time periods or other additional protections for tenants in foreclosure proceedings.","confidence":0.75,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"rent_increase_limits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Prohibition on rent increases above rent control or unconscionable increases","requirement":"The page lists as prohibited conduct an increase in rent in excess of any rent control or rent leveling ordinance, or, where the property is not subject to rent control, an unreasonable or unconscionable rent increase.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Rent-controlled or rent-leveling properties, and where no rent control applies, other residential rentals covered by the provision.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":null},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"Truth in Renting Statement (N.J. Dep't of Cmty. Affairs)","quoted_span":"An increase in rent in excess of any rent control or rent leveling ordinance, or if the property is not subject to rent control, an unreasonable or unconscionable rent increase.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.4,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
